      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weston%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Hook & Hastings Company organ factory, Weston’s largest industry, moved to North Avenue in 1888 and employed many residents until the Great Depression.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weston%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Two taverns of great historical importance survive today: the Josiah Smith Tavern and the Golden Ball Tavern, now a museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weston%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Weston boasts 37 scenic roads, defined by Massachusetts law, preserving the town’s semi‑rural charm.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weston%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "More than 2,000 acres—18 percent of Weston’s land—are kept as parks, wetlands, and forests, offering 90 miles of trails.",
